WEBVTT

00:00:00.950 --> 00:00:03.538
Most social apps are built to keep you scrolling.

00:00:03.587 --> 00:00:06.346
Misfits was designed to get you out of the house.

00:00:07.196 --> 00:00:10.581
It’s a community app where you find people who share your interests,

00:00:10.581 --> 00:00:12.721
through clubs, and then actually meet them.

00:00:12.771 --> 00:00:17.228
Keerthi designed it from research to a full high-fidelity app,

00:00:17.228 --> 00:00:20.104
in five weeks, alongside other projects.

00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:23.822
He started with a survey of more than two

00:00:23.822 --> 00:00:26.772
hundred people about how they make friends today,

00:00:26.772 --> 00:00:28.142
followed by interviews.

00:00:28.992 --> 00:00:30.617
Two findings kept coming up.

00:00:30.667 --> 00:00:33.245
Online connection felt inauthentic:

00:00:33.245 --> 00:00:36.855
people didn’t trust profiles and follower counts.

00:00:36.905 --> 00:00:39.400
And the leap from chatting to meeting in

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:42.332
person was where most connections quietly died.

00:00:43.582 --> 00:00:45.919
That led to three decisions.

00:00:45.969 --> 00:00:48.819
First: clubs, not profiles.

00:00:48.869 --> 00:00:52.081
The home of the app is a club built around an interest,

00:00:52.081 --> 00:00:56.226
because a shared interest is a reason to talk that doesn’t feel forced.

00:00:57.076 --> 00:00:59.651
Second: show the club before you commit.

00:00:59.701 --> 00:01:02.129
Joining a group of strangers is a risk,

00:01:02.129 --> 00:01:05.241
so the club page answers the real questions first:

00:01:05.241 --> 00:01:10.104
when it meets, who runs it, who’s in it, and what a meetup actually looks like.

00:01:10.954 --> 00:01:13.666
Third: every path ends in an event.

00:01:13.716 --> 00:01:17.103
Chat exists to make plans, and anyone can host one,

00:01:17.103 --> 00:01:21.486
because the people who organise are the ones who feel they belong.

00:01:22.736 --> 00:01:28.638
In five weeks: research and personas, then flows from wireframes to high fidelity,

00:01:28.638 --> 00:01:33.028
then usability sessions, and fixes wherever people hesitated.

00:01:34.278 --> 00:01:38.368
The result is a complete high-fidelity app: onboarding,

00:01:38.368 --> 00:01:41.641
club discovery, club pages, chat and events.

00:01:41.691 --> 00:01:45.386
It was a design project, so there are no usage numbers to show.

00:01:46.636 --> 00:01:47.489
Looking back,

00:01:47.489 --> 00:01:52.148
Keerthi would decide how success is measured before designing anything.

00:01:52.198 --> 00:01:56.773
For this app, that’s how many people who join a club go to its first event.

00:01:56.823 --> 00:02:02.256
And he’d test the scary part in real life, with a real meetup, not just screens.

00:02:03.106 --> 00:02:05.094
Design by Keerthi Vardhan.

